<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biswa Singha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1515–<a href="/years/1540">1540</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Biswa Singha was the founder of the Koch kingdom in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in the early 16th century. Uniting Koch chiefs after the fall of the Kamata kingdom, he made his capital at Kamatapur and promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, inviting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> to his court. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara-narayan">Nara Narayan</a> raised the kingdom to its height, and his line ruled Cooch Behar until 1949.</p></div>
